# Data Quality Intelligence System — Telco Customer Churn (IBM)

A small end-to-end project exploring how to profile and validate a real-world dataset **before** cleaning it — starting with manual human investigation, then translating those findings into automated checks (Power Query / M), and finally comparing the two.

## 🎯 Project Goal

Most data cleaning tutorials jump straight to "fill nulls, drop duplicates." This project asks a different question first:

> **Is this actually a data quality problem, or is it explainable by the structure of the data?**

Example: 11 missing values in `TotalCharges` looked like a data quality issue at first glance — until cross-checking against `tenure` showed all 11 belonged to brand-new customers with zero tenure. Not an error. Just customers who hadn't been billed yet.

## 🗂️ Repository Structure

```
telco_churn_data_IBM/
└── Human Investigation/
    ├── 01_Raw_Data/       # Original, untouched dataset (IBM Telco Customer Churn)
    ├── Data_Profile/       # Excel dashboard: row/column counts, data types, missing cell summary
    └── notes/              # Manual investigation log (findings, evidence, interpretation, status)
```

## 🔍 Phase 1 — Human Investigation (current)

Before writing a single cleaning rule, each column was manually reviewed and logged with:

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Findings** | What was observed |
| **Evidence** | The data backing the finding |
| **Interpretation** | Why it happened |
| **Status** | No issue / Explainable / Needs review |

**Checks performed:**
- Duplicate `CustomerID` values
- Missing value patterns (and *why* they're missing)
- Categorical value consistency
- Logical relationships between dependent columns (e.g. `InternetService` ↔ related service columns, `PhoneService` ↔ `MultipleLines`)

**Key findings so far:**

| Column | Status | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| Customer ID | No issue | 7,043 unique customers, no duplicates |
| Total Charges | Explainable | 11 missing values all have 0 tenure — not yet billed |
| Churn Reason | Explainable | 5,174 missing values correspond to customers who did not churn |
| Categorical columns | No issue | No inconsistent category labels found |
| Internet Service ↔ related services | No issue | Customers without internet correctly show "No internet service" for dependent fields |
| Phone Service ↔ Multiple Lines | No issue | Logic between phone service and line count is consistent |

## 🛠️ Phase 2 — Automated Data Quality Checks (next)

Translate the manual rules above into **Power Query / M** logic that outputs a *reason*, not just a flag — e.g.:

```m
if [tenure] = 0 and [TotalCharges] = null then "Explainable - new customer"
else if [TotalCharges] = null then "Needs review"
else "OK"
```

Then compare automated results against the manual investigation to check for gaps.

## 📊 Phase 3 — Dashboard

An interactive Excel dashboard (`Data_Profile/`) summarizing:
- Total rows / columns
- Column data types (Text, Number, Decimal, Category, Binary)
- Missing cell distribution by column
- Pivot table for deeper drill-down

## 🧰 Tools

- Excel (Power Query, Pivot Tables, Dashboard)
- Power Query / M (planned automation layer)

## 📁 Dataset

[IBM Telco Customer Churn](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/blastchar/telco-customer-churn) — public dataset, 7,043 rows × 33 columns.

